#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a54968-0216-55c2-9a8a-0bcdf4fca7f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133269} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3b910c-ed49-5562-b5b5-3c07647d2ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133270} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc36f7c-b92f-5126-9272-ffb91d12ee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133271} {:answer/id #uuid "c6db85b6-61f2-5ef6-aaca-a1350ca39d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133272} {:answer/id #uuid "3027af17-4f03-5cb9-83dc-c7b31c856e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac3b910c-ed49-5562-b5b5-3c07647d2ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133270} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b987768-1cf1-5774-a3e1-0eb5e6a4f663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f8ab8c-12d7-5df5-b151-ee8250c362bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96833" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d58baa-e21b-5238-bfbc-4b8a220e69f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308193} {:answer/id #uuid "c7db8835-10e4-594f-8c15-e31889afbcbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308194} {:answer/id #uuid "e62994ca-1b05-5590-96bb-9d47fecb5e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308195} {:answer/id #uuid "e69e99b6-7a4a-5d21-9ac4-0af246639467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308196} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c71d9f-f467-5a00-a274-0f740c6285e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e62994ca-1b05-5590-96bb-9d47fecb5e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308195} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b895dc-840f-5c35-a946-a03d2571ca15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d2a3e3d-fac2-529b-bde6-b9bc5f4517f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97159" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,6b+c⟩$ if   \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=2+3\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=3+8\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,6b+c⟩ & =⟨a,6b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{6}⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =6(\\overset{―}{2+3\\text{i}})+(3+8\\text{i}) \\\\  & =6(2-3\\text{i})+(3+8\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-18\\text{i}+3+8\\text{i} \\\\  & =15-10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff61cd9-1ab9-54da-985c-eefda8afbcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308221} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee09841-ed7c-52fd-a1d1-e33781fa5cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308222} {:answer/id #uuid "abed3f46-9bbc-5fa9-98cd-3999d7a7e37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308223} {:answer/id #uuid "f025f391-8994-5de9-83cd-c7de8ad63ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308224} {:answer/id #uuid "db1313f3-9453-575a-b454-ecf9b7fde1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff61cd9-1ab9-54da-985c-eefda8afbcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308221} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf0700f2-8363-5aac-9ec1-f258333daceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0668cc2a-2453-5ec6-b822-8bc3dff7632f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97215" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2+4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=-5-3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨6a,c⟩+⟨4b,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =6(\\overset{―}{2+4\\text{i}})+4\\overset{―}{(-5-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =6(2-4\\text{i})+4(-5+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-24\\text{i}-20+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =-8-12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5381d251-9885-5c9e-a699-72f24285757a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308235} {:answer/id #uuid "577b9a0c-91bd-56d1-af63-ec819078319d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308236} {:answer/id #uuid "48e0a8b7-7260-5a55-b4d8-0d92c52c3651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308237} {:answer/id #uuid "845d7f5f-8b5c-5dc3-9a52-ac98c1f2061a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308238} {:answer/id #uuid "cc573856-49c5-5193-a781-d378667321ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "577b9a0c-91bd-56d1-af63-ec819078319d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308236} :answer-field/id #uuid "effef674-0dac-5630-a8b8-2d53c8518066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c55ff0e-5512-5272-a32d-49392f4f28c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97258" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
